    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper measures the dimensionality and domain transfer of deception-related probe signals, but does not test sparse-feature superposition, capacity-driven polysemanticity, SAE feature completeness, or the dimensionality of superposed features.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports results about deception-probe representations that are adjacent to representation geometry, but they do not measure the superposition or feature properties named by any claim version. The rows below record those related results as versions none. Cross-domain transfer and style-robustness findings are not included because they do not measure superposition geometry or feature dimensionality.",
    "test_reason": "The study trains deception probes on Gemma 3 activations and tests deception-geometry hypotheses: single direction, subspace, cone, and entropy proxy. It never measures superposition, polysemantic neurons, capacity, SAE or dictionary features, or the dimensionality of superposed features, and it does not cite the original source. Its 'distributed sub-threshold features' language refers to PCA components of deception-related activations. These are not superposed features. No row bears on V1–V6.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors reject the single-direction and entropy-proxy hypotheses, and find per-domain k*=0, with the signal spread over many weak dimensions. They conclude style fragility is a training-distribution artifact that style augmentation fixes (held-out AUROC 0.979–0.983). They also caveat that the probes may detect the deceptive prompt regime rather than actual deceptive output.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper trains and evaluates deception probes; it does not apply or reproduce the Toy Models of Superposition method.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Probe performance varies with PCA dimensionality; cross-domain transfer varies by model scale and dataset. Style augmentation changes robustness, but is outside the claim versions.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "PCA-restricted deception probes gain AUROC as k rises: k=1 gives 0.609–0.804, k=5 gives 0.908–0.990, k=50 gives 0.994–0.999. A full single-vector linear probe gives 0.998–1.000.",
        "proposed_claim": "In Gemma 3 1B–27B, prompt-induced deception is linearly decodable, but not from the top-variance principal component alone.",
